# Java sources are always text, whatever bytes they happen to contain.
#
# A single raw NUL inside a character literal is legal Java (JLS 3.10.4) and compiles and behaves
# correctly, but it makes git classify the whole file as binary: `git diff`, `git show -p`,
# `git log -p` and every review UI built on them print "Binary files ... differ" instead of a diff,
# and `git grep` prints "Binary file ... matches" instead of the matching line. That happened to
# use-core/src/test/java/org/tzi/use/uncertainty/differential/AcceptedThrowPairs.java -- the one
# class in the differential harness that can turn a pair of throws into an agreement, and whose only
# safeguard is that a human reads the written sign-off.
#
# `diff` (without `text`) changes how git *renders* these paths and nothing about how it stores or
# checks them out: no end-of-line normalisation is requested here, so no committed file changes.
*.java diff
